Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Create a stringstream to hold the HTML string
    std::stringstream htmlStream;

    // Write the markdown text to the stringstream
    htmlStream << markdown;

    // Return the HTML string
    return htmlStream.str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a sample markdown text";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to write the markdown text to it, which is then converted to HTML using the `<<` operator. The resulting HTML string is then returned.

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and does not contain any special characters or syntax that would cause issues with the conversion. If you need to handle more complex markdown text, you may want to use a more sophisticated approach, such as using a library like `cpp-markdown` to convert the markdown to HTML.

